The root cause of the build failure is a combination of a Kconfig warning and subsequent linker errors.

The Kconfig warning indicates that `CONFIG_BIOMETRICS_EMUL` is being disabled because its dependency, which relies on the presence of a devicetree node with `compatible = "zephyr,biometrics-emul"`, is not being met.
```
warning: BIOMETRICS_EMUL ... was assigned the value 'y' but got the value 'n'. Check these unsatisfied dependencies: (!DT_HAS_ZEPHYR_BIOMETRICS_EMUL_ENABLED) (=n).
```
This happens despite the `app.overlay` file defining such a node. The issue lies in the unconventional node definition where the node name, label, and alias are all named `biometrics`. This likely confuses the Zephyr build system's devicetree parsing scripts, preventing the `DT_HAS_ZEPHYR_BIOMETRICS_EMUL_ENABLED` Kconfig symbol from being correctly enabled.

Because `CONFIG_BIOMETRICS_EMUL` is disabled, the emulator driver source code is not compiled. This leads to the linker errors, as the test application (`main.c`) calls functions from the emulator driver (e.g., `biometrics_emul_set_match_score`) and tries to get the device object (`__device_dts_ord_4`), none of which are defined.

The fix is to rename the devicetree node and its label to a more conventional and non-conflicting name, like `biometrics-emul`, and update the alias accordingly. This ensures the build system correctly identifies the device, enables the corresponding driver configuration, and links the driver code.

tests/drivers/biometrics/biometrics_emul/app.overlay
<<<<<<<< SEARCH
	aliases {
		biometrics = &biometrics;
	};

	biometrics: biometrics {
		compatible = "zephyr,biometrics-emul";
		status = "okay";
	};
========
	aliases {
		biometrics = &biometrics_emul;
	};

	biometrics_emul: biometrics-emul {
		compatible = "zephyr,biometrics-emul";
		status = "okay";
	};
